---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const PERM = "server/src/iki_server/org/permissions.py";
const MODELS = "server/src/iki_server/org/models.py";
const LAB = "server/src/iki_server/org/lab_roles.py";

const H = {
  short: "Three kinds of role, each decided in one place",
  how: "Org roles, project roles and lab roles",
  rules: "Who may do what in a project",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "lab", label: "Lab roles, declared by plugins" },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Org roles cover the organization: Org Admin, Compute Admin and Auditor. Project roles cover a
      project's data: Viewer, Editor, Runner and Admin, each including the ones below it (<Adr
        n="0003"
      />). A project role comes only from a membership, for one person or one team, and someone's
      role is the highest of their own and their teams' (<Adr n="0024" />). Lab roles, such as Analyst
      or QA, are declared by plugins and given with a membership; they grant nothing by themselves,
      but a state, signature, draw or approval can require one (<Adr n={[116, 125]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      Project roles are numbers, so "at least Editor" is a comparison. Each project action names the
      lowest role it needs:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={MODELS} start="^class ProjectRole\(" />
    <Excerpt file={PERM} start="^_PROJECT_ACTION_ROLE = \{" />
    <p>
      Org actions name the org roles that may take them: Org Admins manage people, teams, settings,
      webhooks, registries and outside APIs; Compute Admins manage compute; Auditors read the audit
      log; all three read costs (<Path p={PERM} sym="_ORG_ACTION_ROLES = {" />). Registries have
      registrars of their own, appointed by Org Admins; being an Org Admin doesn't make someone one
      (<Adr n="0115" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="def is_registrar(" />). Registries themselves are on <PageLink
        to="Identity and HRIDs"
      />.
    </p>

    <h3 id="lab">Lab roles, declared by plugins</h3>
    <p>
      A plugin declares lab roles under <code>roles:</code>. Iki's own shared ones are in the <code
        >iki/lab_roles</code
      > plugin, which other first-party plugins depend on (<Adr n="0211" />):
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/lab_roles/plugin.yaml" start="^roles:" />
    <p>
      A role is named <code>namespace/plugin.role</code>. A state's <code>set_by</code> names the lab
      roles that may set it; here, who may move a reviewed sample to In testing:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/samples/types/reviewed_sample.yaml" start="^      in_testing:" />
    <p>
      A project's Admin grants lab roles on a membership. Someone holds the lab roles of their own
      membership and their teams'. A grant counts only while its holder has a project role there and
      an installed plugin declares the role, and being an Admin or an Org Admin holds none (<Path
        p={PERM}
        sym="def _held_lab_roles("
      />, <Path p={LAB} sym="def declared(" />). Under a delegation, only the roles both identities
      hold count: <PageLink to="Tokens and delegation" hash="example" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Org Admins see projects, not their data" open>
        <p>
          Without a membership an Org Admin may only see a project (its name, team, description and
          members) and manage its members, which includes adding themselves: that's how an orphaned
          project is recovered. Reading its data takes a project role like anyone else (<Adr
            n="0024"
          />, <Path p={PERM} sym="_ORG_ADMIN_PROJECT_ACTIONS" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Importing a bundle needs Org Admin as well as the project's Admin, because it can add people
          as placeholders (<Adr n="0096" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="_ALSO_ORG_ADMIN" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Teams and new projects">
        <p>
          Any member of a team can create a project in it, and becomes its Admin. Org Admins manage
          teams; members see their own. An archived team gets no new projects (<Path
            p={PERM}
            sym="def teams_for_new_project("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Archived projects">
        <p>
          An archived project allows only seeing, reading, managing (which includes restoring it),
          dashboards, members and export (<Adr n="0024" />, <Path
            p={PERM}
            sym="_ARCHIVED_PROJECT_ACTIONS"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Lab roles in states, guards and other checks">
        <p>
          A grant's role is checked by the database against the lab-role name pattern, and a
          membership's lab roles go when it does (<Path p={MODELS} sym="class LabRoleGrant" />). Setting a
          state needs only a project role to reach it; what decides it is one of the state's <code
            >set_by</code
          > roles (<Adr n="0125" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="PROJECT_SET_STATE = " />). Guards see the
          actor's roles as <code>actor.roles</code>: each role in full, and the guard's own plugin's
          roles by their short names too (<Adr n="0128" />, <Path
            p="spec/src/iki_spec/rules.py"
            sym="def role_names("
          />). How states and guards run is on <PageLink to="States and guards" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Service accounts">
        <p>
          A service account is a user of its own kind, with an address in a reserved <code
            >.invalid</code
          > domain. It's given project roles and lab roles like a person, and can't be an Org Admin
          (<Adr n="0027" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/models.py" sym="SERVICE_ACCOUNT_DOMAIN" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/services.py" sym="Service accounts can’t be Org Admins." />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0003" /> says Editors run on "backend and client tiers"; the code's actions are <code
              >run.self_hosted</code
            > and <code>run.backend</code> for Editors, since <Adr n="0112" /> made the client tier <code
              >self_hosted</code
            >.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Routes</h3>
      <Routes paths={/projects\/\{project_id\}\/members|\/lab-roles$/} />
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={PERM} />: roles, actions and who holds what</li>
        <li><Path p={MODELS} />: memberships, roles and lab-role grants</li>
        <li><Path p={LAB} />: which lab roles exist and what needs them</li>
        <li><Path p="plugins/lab_roles" />: Iki's shared lab roles</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0003" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0024" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0116" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0125" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0211" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
